      "type": "text",
      "text": "<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 44 / 72>"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "An engineering design package, including process flow diagrams (PFD), piping and instrumentation\r\ndiagrams (P&ID), and reactor layout drawings, is provided as supporting documentation.\r\n1. Reactor Dimensions and Configuration\r\nThe reactor design documentation includes:\r\nOverall reactor length, diameter, and wall thickness;\r\nFeed inlet and biochar discharge outlet positions;\r\nGas outlet and combustion chamber interface;\r\nInsulation layers and refractory lining specifications;\r\nExternal heat transfer and combustion systems.\r\nMaterial inflows and outflows are clearly indicated, including:\r\nBiomass feed input;\r\nSyngas output;\r\nBiochar discharge;\r\nFlue gas exhaust.\r\n2. Monitoring and Control Systems\r\nThe engineering diagram identifies:\r\nTemperature sensors (multiple zones along reactor length);\r\nPressure monitoring points;\r\nGas flow measurement locations;\r\nSafety relief systems;\r\nControl valves and actuators.\r\nSensor positioning ensures continuous monitoring of critical parameters for:\r\nPyrolysis temperature control;\r\nPrevention of oxygen intrusion;\r\nSafe gas combustion.\r\nPROJECT DESIGN 44 / 72"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 45 / 72>"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "3. Internal and External Equipment\r\nInternal equipment details include:\r\nReactor screw or conveying system (if applicable);\r\nInternal heat distribution configuration;\r\nInsulation systems.\r\nExternal equipment included in the design:\r\nGas combustion chamber or thermal oxidizer;\r\nCyclone or particulate separation unit (if applicable);\r\nHeat exchanger (if applicable);\r\nCooling system for biochar discharge;\r\nEmission stack.\r\nAll essential process equipment necessary for safe and effective operation is identified in the\r\nengineering documentation.\r\n4. Viewpoints and Layout Representation\r\nThe engineering documentation includes multiple viewpoints, including:\r\nPlan view;\r\nSide elevation;\r\nCross-sectional view;\r\nProcess flow schematic.\r\nThese views clearly demonstrate the positioning of key components and monitoring instrumentation.\r\nhttps://www.bestongroup.net/wp-content/uploads/2021/11/化双炉流程导示图（中英）.gif\r\nFILES\r\nBST-30_Working_Flow_Chart.pdf, Layout_BST-30.pdf, specifications_BST-30.pdf\r\nPROJECT DESIGN 45 / 72"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 46 / 72>"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "E.3\r\nProjects must describe and evidence the sensors used to quantify any loss of pyrolysis\r\ngasses during operation of the reactor to leakage.\r\nSUBMISSION\r\nE.3 – Sensors Used to Quantify Pyrolysis\r\nGas Leakage\r\nR-SZK5-0\r\nThe Project uses a BST-30 continuous rotary carbonization reactor (304 stainless steel) operating\r\nunder slightly negative pressure conditions with integrated gas recovery and sealed combustion\r\nsystemQuotation of BST-30(304ss) with….\r\nThe design incorporates engineered sealing systems, negative pressure operation, water seal devices,\r\nexplosion relief systems, and continuous PLC-based monitoring to prevent and detect unintended gas\r\nleakage.\r\n1. Flue Gas Outflow Monitoring (G-G21Y-0)\r\nThe BST-30 includes:\r\nHigh-pressure centrifugal fan (explosion-proof, rated up to 600°C) Quotation of BST-30(304ss)\r\nwith…\r\nBoiler centrifugal induced draft fan (standard emission system) Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…\r\nSealed combustion chamber with negative pressure design Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…\r\nGas flow is driven through controlled induced draft systems, ensuring all produced syngas is directed\r\nto combustion and not released to atmosphere.\r\nOutflow monitoring is performed through:\r\nCombustion chamber exhaust monitoring\r\nDraft fan performance monitoring\r\nContinuous pressure monitoring of the system\r\nThese parameters allow reconciliation between expected syngas generation and measured\r\ncombustion output.\r\nPROJECT DESIGN 46 / 72"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 47 / 72>"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "2. Reactor Model and Performance Characterization (G-QBS8-0)\r\nThe BST-30 is a continuous-type rotary furnace carbonization system operating with:\r\nIndirect heating (biomass does not contact flame) Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…\r\nWeak oxygen environment Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…\r\nContinuous feeding and discharge\r\nThe reactor performance is characterized using:\r\nMeasured biomass input rate (1–1.5 t/h) Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…\r\nContinuous screw feeding with frequency conversion control Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…\r\nContinuous screw discharge with water-cooling jacket Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…\r\nMass balance is based on:\r\nBiomass Input = Biochar Output + Syngas Combusted + Minor Process Losses\r\n3. Chemical Kinetics Consideration (G-YNGJ-0)\r\nThe BST-30 operates under controlled thermal radiation heating in a rotary furnace under oxygen￾limited conditionsQuotation of BST-30(304ss) with….\r\nAlthough the manufacturer does not provide a detailed kinetic equation model, the system:\r\nControls temperature via PLC system Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…\r\nMaintains weak oxygen atmosphere\r\nRecovers syngas after initial 1–1.5h preheating phase Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…\r\nGiven industrial continuous operation and fixed temperature range, a steady-state thermochemical\r\nmass balance approach is used instead of a laboratory kinetic model.\r\n4. Model Validation and Mass Balance (G-5Z7W-0)\r\nValidation relies on:\r\nPROJECT DESIGN 47 / 72"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 48 / 72>"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Continuous biomass feed measurement\r\nContinuous screw discharge measurement\r\nSyngas recirculation monitoring\r\nFan performance and draft control\r\nThe system includes:\r\nIntegrated gas recovery system Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…\r\nWater seal device in combustible gas pipeline Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…\r\nCyclone dust removal system Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…\r\nMass balance deviations beyond predefined tolerance trigger operational review.\r\n5. Alternative Leakage Control Measures (G-D8Z6-0)\r\nThe BST-30 integrates multiple leakage prevention systems:\r\nSix-layer sealing system (above industry standard) Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…\r\nSlight negative pressure operation Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…\r\nExplosion-proof relief port Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…\r\nSafety water seal device Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…\r\nNitrogen purging system Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…\r\nThese systems significantly reduce risk of gas escape.\r\n6. Continuous Sub-Atmospheric Pressure Operation (G-MJPG-0)\r\nThe manufacturer specifies that:\r\n\"The entire system is in a slightly negative pressure state.\"Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…\r\nNegative pressure is maintained through:\r\nInduced draft fans\r\nSealed combustion chamber\r\nContinuous pressure monitoring via PLC system Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…\r\nPROJECT DESIGN 48 / 72"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 49 / 72>"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Maintaining sub-atmospheric pressure ensures that any seal imperfection results in inward air flow\r\nrather than outward gas leakage.\r\n7. Regular Leakage Testing (G-VWDT-0)\r\nThe system includes:\r\nQuality inspection standards for industrial metal pipeline engineering Quotation of BST-30(304ss)\r\nwith…\r\nStandards for instrumentation installation and grounding Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…\r\nAnnual leakage integrity inspection will include:\r\nPipeline inspection\r\nSeal inspection\r\nPressure stability verification\r\nWater seal functionality check\r\nTesting is conducted at least once every 12 months and after major maintenance.\r\nEvidence to Upload in Certify\r\nWorking Flow Chart (page 6) Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…\r\nSecurity Design (page 8) Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…\r\nTechnical Parameters (page 4) Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…\r\nSyngas system component list (page 14) Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…\r\nPLC monitoring description (page 11) Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…\r\nFILES\r\nspecifications_BST-30.pdf\r\nPROJECT DESIGN 49 / 72"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 50 / 72>"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "E.4\r\nProjects must describe the selection of materials for each component of the reactor,\r\nincluding suitable justification of these choices from the perspectives of thermal and\r\nmechanical resilience.\r\nSUBMISSION\r\nE.4 – Selection of Reactor Materials and\r\nJustification\r\nR-DMET-0\r\nThe project uses a BST-30 continuous rotary carbonization reactor constructed primarily of 304\r\nstainless steel, as specified in the technical quotation (“BST-30 (304ss)”)Quotation of BST-30(304ss)\r\nwith….\r\nMaterial selection was made based on:\r\nThermal resilience under continuous pyrolysis temperatures\r\nMechanical resistance under rotary operation\r\nCorrosion resistance due to organic acids and condensable vapors\r\nLong-term operational durability in oxygen-limited environments\r\n1. Primary Reactor Body – 304 Stainless Steel\r\nThe rotary carbonization furnace is fabricated in 304 stainless steelQuotation of BST-30(304ss) with….\r\nThermal Justification\r\nPyrolysis operation occurs at elevated temperatures (typically 450–600°C for biomass\r\ncarbonization).\r\n304 stainless steel maintains mechanical strength and structural stability within this temperature\r\nrange.\r\nIndirect heating design reduces localized thermal shock.\r\nChemical Justification\r\nPROJECT DESIGN 50 / 72"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 51 / 72>"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "During biomass pyrolysis, condensable vapors contain organic acids (acetic acid), phenolics, and\r\nlight tars.\r\n304 stainless steel provides corrosion resistance to weak organic acids and condensable gases.\r\nResistance to oxidation under limited oxygen exposure increases reactor longevity.\r\nMechanical Justification\r\nContinuous rotary motion requires:\r\nFatigue resistance\r\nWeld integrity\r\nDimensional stability\r\n304 stainless steel provides adequate tensile strength and weldability for industrial rotary kilns.\r\n2. External Furnace and Combustion Components\r\nThe combustion chamber and flue gas handling components are designed for:\r\nHigh-temperature flue gas (up to 600°C as per fan specification) Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…\r\nContinuous negative pressure operation\r\nStructural stability under thermal cycling\r\nHigh-temperature resistant steel alloys are used in fans and combustion components as specified by\r\nthe manufacturerQuotation of BST-30(304ss) with….\r\n3. Feeding and Discharge Systems\r\nScrew Feeder System\r\nConstructed with reinforced steel components.\r\nDesigned for continuous torque loading.\r\nFrequency-controlled operation reduces mechanical stress.\r\nWater-Cooled Screw Discharge\r\nIncludes water-cooling jacket Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with… .\r\nPROJECT DESIGN 51 / 72"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 52 / 72>"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Thermal gradient is managed to prevent thermal warping.\r\nCooling protects mechanical seals and bearing assemblies.\r\n4. Gas System Components\r\nThe BST-30 includes:\r\nSix-layer sealing system Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…\r\nWater seal device in gas pipeline Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…\r\nExplosion relief port Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…\r\nNitrogen purging system Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…\r\nThese components are designed to:\r\nPrevent gas leakage\r\nResist internal gas pressure variations\r\nMaintain sub-atmospheric system pressure\r\nHigh Pressure Considerations (G-PV0S-0)\r\nThe BST-30 does not operate under high pressure.\r\nThe manufacturer specifies that:\r\n““The entire system is in a slightly negative pressure state.”Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…”\r\nTherefore:\r\nThe reactor is not classified as a high-pressure vessel.\r\nInternal pressure remains below atmospheric pressure.\r\nMechanical integrity requirements differ from pressurized reactors.\r\nBecause the system operates under negative pressure:\r\nRisk of outward vessel rupture due to internal overpressure is minimized.\r\nAny seal imperfection results in inward air ingress rather than outward gas leakage.\r\nPROJECT DESIGN 52 / 72"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 53 / 72>"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Explosion relief port provides additional mechanical safety protectionQuotation of BST-30(304ss)\r\nwith….\r\nCompliance with Pressure Vessel Standards\r\n(G-Y4R3-0 / G-5EFD-0)\r\nSince the BST-30 does not operate as a pressurized vessel:\r\nIt does not fall under high-pressure vessel classification.\r\nIt does not require compliance with Pressure Equipment Directive 2014/68/EU.\r\nInstead, the system is manufactured according to:\r\nIndustrial metal pipeline engineering standards Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…\r\nInstrument installation and grounding standards Quotation of BST-30(304ss) with…\r\nFabrication and installation will comply with applicable industrial equipment and structural safety\r\nstandards in Brazil.\r\nE.5\r\nProjects must describe and evidence an appropriate reactor maintenance plan.\r\nSUBMISSION\r\nE.5 – Reactor Maintenance Plan\r\nR-19AF-0\r\nThe project implements a preventive and corrective maintenance plan for the BST-30 continuous rotary\r\npyrolysis reactor to ensure structural integrity, operational safety and mitigation of potential material\r\nloss events.\r\nThe maintenance plan is structured around:\r\nMechanical integrity of the rotary kiln body (304 stainless steel)\r\nIntegrity of seals and gas containment systems\r\nPROJECT DESIGN 53 / 72"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 54 / 72>"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Monitoring of thermal and mechanical degradation\r\nNegative-pressure system verification\r\nAnnual leakage verification procedures\r\n1. Structural Integrity of the Reactor Vessel\r\nG-CR8Q-0\r\nThe BST-30 operates as a continuous rotary carbonization furnace fabricated in 304 stainless steel\r\nand maintained under slight negative pressure during operation.\r\nTo ensure structural integrity, the following procedures are implemented:\r\n1.1 Routine Visual Inspection (Daily / Weekly)\r\nInspection of outer shell for:\r\nThermal distortion\r\nSurface cracking\r\nCorrosion spots\r\nInspection of welded joints\r\nVerification of flange tightness\r\n1.2 Seal System Inspection\r\nThe reactor includes a six-layer sealing system and water seal in the gas pipeline.\r\nWeekly checks include:\r\nSeal wear inspection\r\nVerification of water seal functionality\r\nReplacement of worn sealing components as required\r\nThis prevents uncontrolled gas escape and mitigates material loss events.\r\n1.3 Bearing and Rotary System Monitoring\r\nMonitoring of bearing temperature\r\nLubrication schedule compliance\r\nPROJECT DESIGN 54 / 72"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 55 / 72>"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Alignment verification of rotary supports\r\nExcessive vibration or misalignment is treated as a stop-condition.\r\n2. Monitoring and Mitigation of Mechanical and Thermal\r\nDegradation\r\nG-7PSP-0\r\nThe following degradation mechanisms are monitored:\r\n2.1 Thermal Fatigue\r\nContinuous monitoring of reactor temperature\r\nGradual heating and cooling protocols to avoid thermal shock\r\nShutdown if abnormal temperature gradients are detected\r\n2.2 Mechanical Fatigue\r\nMonthly inspection of:\r\nSupport rollers\r\nDrive motor\r\nGearbox assembly\r\nReplacement of worn components based on manufacturer schedule\r\n2.3 Corrosion Risk\r\nGiven exposure to pyrolysis vapors containing organic acids:\r\nQuarterly inspection of internal surfaces (during planned downtime)\r\nReplacement or repair of damaged lining if identified\r\n3. Gas Containment and Leakage Prevention\r\nThe system includes:\r\nPROJECT DESIGN 55 / 72"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 56 / 72>"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Slight negative pressure operation\r\nExplosion relief port\r\nNitrogen purging system\r\nWater seal device\r\nPreventive measures include:\r\nContinuous monitoring of draft fan performance\r\nPressure sensor calibration checks (every 6 months)\r\nAnnual leakage test of gas pipeline system\r\nIf:\r\nPressure becomes positive relative to atmosphere\r\nSeal degradation is detected\r\nGas odor or abnormal emission is observed\r\n→ Immediate controlled shutdown is executed.\r\n4. Maintenance Schedule Summary\r\nFrequency Activity\r\nDaily Visual inspection, temperature check\r\nWeekly Seal inspection, lubrication verification\r\nMonthly Mechanical alignment and drive system check\r\nQuarterly Internal inspection (scheduled downtime)\r\nBiannual Sensor calibration\r\nAnnual Comprehensive leakage test + structural audit\r\nAll maintenance actions are logged digitally and stored for audit purposes.\r\n5. Compliance with Applicable Standards\r\nPROJECT DESIGN 56 / 72"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 57 / 72>"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "G-F0H0-0\r\nThe BST-30 reactor does not operate under high pressure and is maintained under slight negative\r\npressure.\r\nTherefore:\r\nIt is not classified as a pressure vessel.\r\nPressure Equipment Directive 2014/68/EU does not apply.\r\nMaintenance complies with:\r\nBrazilian industrial equipment safety regulations\r\nApplicable occupational safety standards (NR-12, NR-13 where applicable to industrial equipment)\r\nManufacturer maintenance manual\r\nPeriodic inspection by a qualified mechanical engineer will be conducted.\r\nSampling procedure\r\nE.6\r\nProjects must describe and justify the sampling procedure, including the number and\r\nfrequency of sampling and analysis.\r\nSUBMISSION\r\nE.6 – Sampling Procedure\r\nR-S8K1-1\r\nThe project implements a statistically robust and representative sampling procedure for biochar\r\nbatches produced by the BST-30 continuous rotary reactor.\r\nThe procedure is designed to:\r\nCapture potential heterogeneity within each production batch\r\nAddress particle size segregation during cooling and storage\r\nEnsure representativity for carbon content and contaminant analysis\r\nPROJECT DESIGN 57 / 72"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 58 / 72>"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Align with internationally recognized sampling practices (IBI / EBC guidance)\r\n1. Definition of Production Batch\r\nA Production Batch is defined as:\r\nContinuous reactor operation under stable temperature and feedstock conditions,\r\nWithin a maximum 24-hour production window, or\r\nMaximum 30 tonnes of biochar (reactor nominal output capacity).\r\nEach batch is uniquely identified by:\r\nDate and time interval\r\nFeedstock lot ID\r\nReactor operating parameters log\r\n2. Sampling Frequency\r\nSampling is conducted:\r\nAt least once per production batch\r\nAdditionally, whenever:\r\nFeedstock changes\r\nReactor temperature profile changes\r\nMaintenance intervention occurs\r\nProcess anomaly is detected\r\nMinimum annual laboratory analysis frequency:\r\n1 full contaminant analysis per 100 tonnes produced\r\n1 proximate/ultimate analysis per batch\r\n3. Sampling Methodology\r\nPROJECT DESIGN 58 / 72"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 59 / 72>"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "3.1 Primary Sampling\r\nBiochar is sampled:\r\nAt the discharge point after cooling screw\r\nPrior to bagging into big-bags\r\nDuring continuous flow\r\nSub-samples are collected:\r\nAt equal time intervals (minimum 5 increments per batch)\r\nAcross different discharge moments\r\nThese increments are combined into a composite sample.\r\n4. Addressing Heterogeneity\r\nG-NP0D-0\r\nHeterogeneity may arise from:\r\nTemperature gradients inside the rotary kiln\r\nParticle size variation\r\nSlight variations in residence time\r\nTo mitigate this:\r\nMultiple incremental samples are taken across the production window.\r\nComposite sample is homogenized mechanically.\r\nQuartering method is applied until laboratory subsample size is achieved.\r\nThis ensures representativity of:\r\nCarbon fraction\r\nAsh content\r\nH/C ratio\r\nContaminants\r\nPROJECT DESIGN 59 / 72"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 60 / 72>"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "5. Horizontal and Vertical Sampling (If Stored)\r\nG-VZQ0-0\r\nIf temporary storage occurs prior to dispatch:\r\nSampling is performed at:\r\nTop layer\r\nMid-layer\r\nBottom layer\r\nIf biochar is stored in bulk pile:\r\nSamples collected from at least 5 spatial points (center + corners).\r\nDepth sampling includes surface and ≥30 cm depth.\r\nThis addresses potential particle segregation during storage or transport.\r\n6. Laboratory Analysis\r\nSamples are analyzed for:\r\nFixed carbon\r\nVolatile matter\r\nAsh\r\nH/Corg ratio\r\nTotal carbon\r\nMoisture\r\nPAHs\r\nHeavy metals\r\npH\r\nLaboratories must:\r\nUse ISO-accredited methods where available.\r\nProvide documented analytical procedures.\r\nPROJECT DESIGN 60 / 72"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 61 / 72>"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "7. Sample Preservation and Traceability\r\nEach composite sample is labeled with batch ID.\r\nRetention sample stored for minimum 12 months.\r\nDigital record includes:\r\nSampling date\r\nOperator\r\nReactor conditions\r\nLab report reference\r\n8. Justification of Sampling Design\r\nThe BST-30 is a continuous rotary reactor operating under:\r\nStable temperature control\r\nContinuous feed\r\nControlled residence time\r\nThis reduces large batch-to-batch variability compared to batch kilns.\r\nTherefore:\r\nTime-based incremental composite sampling is appropriate.\r\nStratified storage sampling is applied when relevant.\r\nFrequency is proportional to production scale and risk profile.\r\nBiochar characterization\r\nE.7\r\nProjects must provide a detailed bulleted list of the relevant standards that have been\r\nutilized in the biochar characterization.\r\nSUBMISSION\r\nPROJECT DESIGN 61 / 72"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 62 / 72>"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "E.7 – Biochar Characterization Standards\r\nR-CXEP-0\r\nThe biochar produced in the BST-30 continuous rotary pyrolysis reactor is characterized using\r\ninternationally recognized analytical standards. All laboratory analyses are conducted by ISO/IEC\r\n17025 accredited laboratories.\r\nThe project applies the +200-year durability classification. Permanence is demonstrated through\r\nelemental composition and H/Corg ratio, without the use of reflectance (R₀) or inertinite fraction\r\nmeasurements.\r\nStandards Utilized for Measuring Biochar Physical Properties\r\nG-39F1-0\r\n• ASTM D1762-84 (Reapproved 2013) – Chemical analysis of wood charcoal (moisture, volatile matter,\r\nash, fixed carbon). • ASTM D3172 – Proximate analysis of carbonaceous materials. • ASTM D7582 –\r\nProximate analysis by thermogravimetric analysis (TGA). • EN 14774-1 – Determination of total moisture\r\ncontent. • EN 14775 – Determination of ash content. • ISO 10390 – Determination of pH. • ISO 11277 –\r\nParticle size distribution (where applicable).\r\nThese standards support determination of fixed carbon content, ash fraction, moisture, and other\r\nphysical parameters relevant to durability and soil application.\r\nStandards Utilized for Measuring Biochar Chemical Properties\r\nG-AK6B-0\r\nDurability-related parameters:\r\n• ASTM D5373 – Ultimate analysis (C, H, N). • EN 15104 – Determination of total carbon, hydrogen and\r\nnitrogen. • H/Corg ratio calculated from elemental analysis results.\r\nContaminant-related parameters (aligned with C.9):\r\nPROJECT DESIGN 62 / 72"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 63 / 72>"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "• EPA Method 3050B / 3051A – Acid digestion for heavy metals. • EPA Method 6020B – ICP-MS\r\nquantification of heavy metals. • EPA Method 8270D – Determination of PAHs (Semi-Volatile Organic\r\nCompounds). • EN 16181 – Determination of PAHs in solid biofuels. • ASTM D4239 – Total sulfur\r\ndetermination (where applicable).\r\nThese chemical characterization standards ensure:\r\n• Demonstration of +200-year permanence via H/Corg. • Verification that heavy metals remain below\r\nregulatory and certification thresholds. • Quantification and control of PAHs prior to soil application.\r\nNo reflectance (R₀) analysis is conducted, as permanence classification relies exclusively on elemental\r\ncomposition and H/Corg ratio in accordance with the +200-year durability pathway.\r\nE.8\r\nProjects should provide the details of any measurements of biochar physical properties\r\nthat have been taken.\r\nSUBMISSION\r\nR-CXEP-0\r\nThe biochar produced by the BST-30 continuous rotary pyrolysis reactor is characterized using\r\ninternationally recognized analytical standards. All laboratory analyses are conducted by ISO/IEC\r\n17025 accredited laboratories.\r\nThe project applies the +200-year durability classification under the Isometric protocol. Permanence is\r\ntherefore demonstrated through H/Corg ratio and elemental composition analysis, without the use of\r\nreflectance (R₀) or inertinite fraction measurements.\r\nStandards Used for Measuring Physical Properties\r\nG-39F1-0\r\n• ASTM D1762-84 (Reapproved 2013) – Chemical analysis of wood charcoal (moisture, volatile matter,\r\nash, fixed carbon).\r\n• ASTM D3172 – Proximate analysis of carbonaceous materials.\r\n• ASTM D7582 – Proximate analysis by thermogravimetric analysis (TGA).\r\n• ASTM D5373 – Ultimate analysis (C, H, N).\r\nPROJECT DESIGN 63 / 72"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 64 / 72>"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "• EN 14774-1 – Determination of total moisture content.\r\n• EN 14775 – Determination of ash content.\r\n• EN 15104 – Determination of total carbon, hydrogen and nitrogen.\r\n• ISO 10390 – Determination of pH.\r\n• H/Corg ratio calculated from elemental analysis results in accordance with European Biochar\r\nCertificate (EBC) and Isometric BiCRS requirements.\r\nThese parameters are used to demonstrate compliance with the +200-year durability class, based on\r\nH/Corg threshold values specified in the protocol.\r\nStandards Used for Measuring Chemical Properties\r\nG-AK6B-0\r\n• EPA Method 3050B / 3051A – Acid digestion for heavy metals analysis.\r\n• EPA Method 6020B – ICP-MS quantification of heavy metals (e.g., Cd, Pb, Cr, Ni, Hg, As).\r\n• EPA Method 8270D – Determination of Semi-Volatile Organic Compounds (including PAHs).\r\n• EN 16181 – Determination of PAHs in solid biofuels.\r\n• ASTM D4239 – Total sulfur determination.\r\n• DIN 38414-S4 – Leaching test for contaminant mobility (where applicable to soil application).\r\nAlignment with Section C.9 (Pollution Prevention)\r\nThe above analytical standards directly support compliance with C.9 by:\r\n• Quantifying total heavy metal concentrations in biochar prior to soil application. • Screening and\r\nquantifying PAHs using internationally recognized analytical methods. • Demonstrating that\r\ncontaminant concentrations remain below applicable regulatory and certification thresholds. •\r\nEnabling traceable laboratory evidence for contaminant control and environmental risk mitigation.\r\nPROJECT DESIGN 64 / 72"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 65 / 72>"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "All contaminant testing results are archived and linked to production batches under the project MRV\r\nsystem.\r\nConsistency Statement\r\nNo reflectance (R₀) or inertinite fraction analysis is used in this project, as permanence classification\r\nrelies exclusively on H/Corg ratio and elemental carbon content consistent with the +200-year\r\ndurability pathway.\r\nE.9\r\nProjects should provide the details of any measurements of biochar chemical properties\r\nthat have been taken.\r\nSUBMISSION\r\nE.9 – Details of Biochar Chemical\r\nMeasurements\r\nR-VGXA-0\r\nAll chemical analyses are performed on biochar produced in the BST-30 continuous rotary pyrolysis\r\nreactor. Laboratory testing is conducted by ISO/IEC 17025 accredited laboratories. Each result is\r\nlinked to a unique production batch ID.\r\nThe project applies the +200-year durability pathway. Permanence is demonstrated through elemental\r\ncomposition and molar ratio analysis without reflectance (R₀) or inertinite fraction measurement.\r\nG-CAR5-0 – Carbon Content Analysis\r\nTotal carbon content is determined via ultimate analysis using:\r\n• ASTM D5373 • EN 15104\r\nMeasured parameter: Total Carbon (% dry basis)\r\nResults are used to quantify stable carbon mass and calculate H/Corg and O/Corg molar ratios.\r\nPROJECT DESIGN 65 / 72"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 66 / 72>"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "G-KMFF-0 – Moisture Content Analysis\r\nMoisture content is determined using:\r\n• EN 14774-1 • ASTM D1762-84\r\nMeasured parameter: Total moisture (% as received)\r\nMoisture is used to convert values to dry basis for durability calculations.\r\nG-TY6S-0 – H/Corg Calculation and Analysis\r\nHydrogen and carbon are measured via elemental analysis.\r\nH/Corg is calculated on a molar basis:\r\nH/Corg = (H/1.008) / (C/12.011)\r\nThe project demonstrates compliance with the +200-year threshold as defined in the Isometric\r\nprotocol.\r\nNo reflectance (R₀) is used.\r\nG-27Y3-0 – O/Corg Calculation and Analysis\r\nOxygen is determined either:\r\n• Directly via elemental analysis, or • By difference (100 − C − H − N − Ash)\r\nO/Corg is calculated on a molar basis and reported for completeness and structural stability\r\nassessment.\r\nG-X6H2-0 – Volatile Matter Analysis\r\nPROJECT DESIGN 66 / 72"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 67 / 72>"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Volatile matter is measured using:\r\n• ASTM D1762-84 • ASTM D7582\r\nMeasured parameter: Volatile matter (% dry basis)\r\nUsed to assess pyrolysis completeness and char stability.\r\nG-4G8C-0 – Fixed Carbon Content Analysis\r\nFixed carbon is determined by proximate analysis:\r\nFixed Carbon = 100 − (Moisture + Volatile Matter + Ash)\r\nMeasured via ASTM D1762-84.\r\nFixed carbon is reported for each batch.\r\nG-BSZP-0 – Ash Content Analysis\r\nAsh content is measured using:\r\n• EN 14775 • ASTM D1762-84\r\nMeasured parameter: Ash (% dry basis)\r\nAsh values are used in both durability and soil application assessment.\r\nG-K3Q0-0 – PAHs (USEPA 16) Analysis\r\nSum of PAH16 is measured using:\r\n• EPA 8270D • EN 16181\r\nTotal PAH concentration is compared against:\r\n• EBC limits • Applicable regulatory thresholds\r\nPROJECT DESIGN 67 / 72"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 68 / 72>"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "This directly supports compliance with Section C.9.\r\nG-TDEA-0 – pH Analysis\r\npH is determined using:\r\n• ISO 10390\r\nMeasured parameter: pH (H₂O extraction)\r\npH is reported for soil application suitability.\r\nG-1Q5M-0 – Cation Exchange Capacity (CEC)\r\nCEC is determined using:\r\n• Ammonium acetate method (ISO 11260 or equivalent)\r\nCEC is reported as supporting soil health co-benefits.\r\nG-90WY-0 – Bulk Carbon Bonding State Analysis\r\nBulk carbon bonding state analysis (e.g., solid-state 13C NMR) is not performed.\r\nJustification:\r\nThe project applies the +200-year durability classification, which does not require bonding state\r\ncharacterization when H/Corg thresholds are met.\r\nDurability is demonstrated via elemental ratios in accordance with protocol requirements.\r\nG-GAM8-0 – External Surface Carbon Bonding State Composition\r\nPROJECT DESIGN 68 / 72"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 69 / 72>"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "External surface carbon bonding state analysis (e.g., XPS) is not performed.\r\nJustification:\r\nSurface bonding state characterization is not required under the +200-year pathway when molar ratio\r\nthresholds are met and pyrolysis conditions are controlled.\r\nMeasurement Frequency\r\n• Elemental, proximate, ash, moisture: every production batch • PAHs and heavy metals: every batch\r\nprior to soil deployment • pH and CEC: representative batch testing per production campaign\r\nSampling is conducted according to E.6.\r\nConsistency Statement\r\nThe chemical measurements described above:\r\n• Fully support the +200-year durability pathway • Do not rely on reflectance (R₀) • Align with\r\ncontamination controls described in C.9 • Are batch-traceable within the MRV system\r\nE.10\r\nProjects must report the analytical laboratory/laboratories that have been utilized for the\r\nbiochar characterization.\r\nSUBMISSION\r\nR-VGXA-0\r\nThe Project measures and documents all relevant chemical properties of biochar produced by the BST￾30 continuous rotary pyrolysis reactor to demonstrate durability, environmental safety, and compliance\r\nwith Isometric BiCRS requirements.\r\nAll analyses are conducted by ISO/IEC 17025 accredited laboratories and are batch-linked through\r\nthe project MRV system.\r\nPROJECT DESIGN 69 / 72"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 70 / 72>"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "1. Permanence-Related Chemical Properties (+200-Year Durability)\r\nThe Project targets the +200-year durability class. Permanence is demonstrated without reflectance\r\n(R₀) or inertinite fraction measurements.\r\nThe following parameters are measured for each production batch:\r\n• Total Carbon (C) • Hydrogen (H) • Nitrogen (N) • H/Corg ratio (calculated from elemental analysis) •\r\nVolatile Matter • Fixed Carbon • Ash content\r\nAnalytical methods:\r\n• ASTM D5373 – Ultimate analysis • ASTM D1762-84 – Proximate analysis • EN 15104 – Elemental\r\ncomposition\r\nDurability is demonstrated by:\r\n• H/Corg below the +200-year threshold defined in the protocol • High fixed carbon content •\r\nControlled volatile fraction\r\nNo reflectance (R₀) analysis is performed.\r\n2. Heavy Metals Analysis (Aligned with C.9)\r\nEach batch is tested for total heavy metal concentrations prior to soil application.\r\nMetals analyzed include:\r\n• Cadmium (Cd) • Lead (Pb) • Chromium (Cr) • Nickel (Ni) • Mercury (Hg) • Arsenic (As) • Copper (Cu) •\r\nZinc (Zn)\r\nAnalytical methods:\r\n• EPA 3050B / 3051A – Acid digestion • EPA 6020B – ICP-MS quantification\r\nResults are compared against:\r\n• Local regulatory thresholds (Brazilian CONAMA standards where applicable) • European Biochar\r\nCertificate upper bound limits • Isometric pathway safety criteria\r\nBiochar is not deployed if concentrations exceed applicable limits.\r\nPROJECT DESIGN 70 / 72"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 71 / 72>"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "3. PAHs and Organic Contaminants (Aligned with C.9)\r\nThe following are measured:\r\n• Total PAH16 (EPA priority list) • Individual PAH species • Sulfur (where applicable)\r\nAnalytical methods:\r\n• EPA 8270D – Semi-volatile organic compounds • EN 16181 – PAH determination\r\nOperational mitigation:\r\nThe BST-30 reactor operates under controlled temperature ranges (typically 450–650°C), with\r\ncontinuous rotation and controlled residence time, minimizing incomplete pyrolysis and PAH formation.\r\nIf PAH concentrations exceed applicable thresholds, the batch is withheld from soil application and\r\ninvestigated.\r\n4. Batch Traceability and Documentation\r\nEach batch of biochar:\r\n• Is assigned a unique batch ID • Is linked to reactor operating parameters (temperature, residence\r\ntime) • Is linked to feedstock source documentation • Is archived with full laboratory certificate\r\nCertificates are retained for audit and MRV verification purposes.\r\n5. Measurement Frequency\r\n• Elemental and proximate analysis: every production batch • Heavy metals: every batch prior to\r\ndeployment • PAHs: every batch prior to deployment\r\nSampling follows the procedure described in E.6.\r\n6. Integration with Reactor Operation (BST-30)\r\nPROJECT DESIGN 71 / 72"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "<PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 72 / 72>"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Chemical measurement data are cross-checked against:\r\n• Reactor temperature logs • Residence time • Feedstock type • System pressure\r\nThis ensures chemical properties are consistent with stable pyrolysis performance and durability\r\nclassification.\r\n7. Consistency Statement\r\nThe project:\r\n• Demonstrates +200-year permanence via H/Corg • Does not rely on R₀ or reflectance • Fully aligns\r\ncontaminant testing with C.9 • Maintains full batch traceability\r\nPROJECT DESIGN 72 / 72"
    }
